# Line endings.
#
# The repo is edited on Windows and deployed to Linux, and git's default is to
# store whatever it was handed. A shell script committed with CRLF fails on the
# instance in a way that reads as nonsense:
#
#     ./setup.sh: /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# The interpreter name really does include the carriage return, so nothing on
# the box can run it and the error names a file that appears to exist. Force LF
# in the repository for everything that Linux executes or parses.

*.sh            text eol=lf
start           text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.timer         text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf

# Firmware is read by the Arduino IDE on Windows and by nothing on Linux, so it
# is left to the platform default.
*.ino           text
